# Negative control for round-2 finding JF-7 — produced 2026-09-17 on Darwin arm64.
#
# JF-7: revision 2's evidence/check-r2.sh fed four checks (JF4a, JF4b, JF5a,
# JF5b) from the tail of a pipeline, so their ok/no mutated pass/fail in a
# subshell. A FAIL among those four would print into the log and still leave
# fail=0, so the closing `[ "$fail" -eq 0 ]` could not trip. This control forces
# exactly that failure in both scripts and records what each does.
#
# Procedure, reproducible as written (neither committed script is edited):
#   d=$(mktemp -d); git archive HEAD | tar -x -C "$d"
#   R="$d/research/runs/R55/2026-09-17T020714Z-62a04ad8fee1/review/evidence"
#   sed -i '' "s/'click.Path(exists=True) parity: a usage error, exit 2.'/'THIS NEEDLE IS NOT IN THE FILE - negative control'/" "$R/<script>"
#   "$R/<script>"; echo "exit=$?"
# The substitution replaces JF5b's needle with text absent from ts config.ts:154,
# so JF5b must fail. Everything else in both scripts is untouched.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# check-r2.sh (revision 2, the defect) — the gate does NOT trip:
FAIL JF5b: ts config.ts:154 is the click.Path(exists=True) parity comment, not :153 — /usr/bin/grep counted 0 occurrences of: THIS NEEDLE IS NOT IN THE FILE - negative control
=== summary ===
PASS=18 FAIL=0 SKIP=0
exit=0
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# check-r3.sh (revision 3, repaired) — the gate DOES trip:
FAIL JF5b: ts config.ts:154 is the click.Path(exists=True) parity comment, not :153 — /usr/bin/grep counted 0 occurrences of: THIS NEEDLE IS NOT IN THE FILE - negative control
=== summary ===
PASS=28 FAIL=1 SKIP=0
exit=1
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Same mutation, same failing check, same FAIL line in both logs. Revision 2
# reports FAIL=0 and exits 0; revision 3 reports FAIL=1 and exits 1. That is the
# whole of JF-7, demonstrated rather than asserted.
